## Something ELSE found, both questions

1. **`fable_queue.md` numbers two different batches `7n`.**  Lines 2330/2336/2342 are `#### 7n-1`, `7n-2`, `7n-3` inside the Alpha TBI row (`alphaswpctxreg`, "Ordering -- the input to the existing ranking has changed"); lines 2472-2519 are the `### 7n-1..7n-5` of this batch.  A reader or tool resolving "7n-3" lands on the Alpha question first.  Rename one batch.
2. **An unread review on `ppcrlwhighhalf`.**  `regress/artifacts/codexfarm_20260926/codex_ppcrlwhighhalf.txt` (14,478 B, tracked since 1202d342 on 10-02) is recorded in NO ledger row, CHANGELOG block, queue entry or receipt (`grep` over all five = 0).  It carries the witness spec used above, the stale-title/MED findings, the line-drift corrections (decoder now `:4535`, `rlwimi`'s conditional at `:2639`), and the scope correction that the two base functions are `rlwnm` then `rlwinm`.  The carrier's unread-review class, fourth instance.
3. **Two committed `mask32` with opposite semantics at HEAD** (`regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py:158` low-32-confined; `regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py:156` 64-bit Book I) -- the 09-11 "nine-day fuse", 28 days old, not addressed by the 10-08 exemption re-date.
4. **The derivation artifact is untracked**: `regress/artifacts/ppcwitnessmaskoracle/` is `??`, so the only thing attesting that the oracle re-pin preceded `#527` is an mtime on a file git has never seen.
5. **`ppcrlwhighhalf` has no `entries` array at all** (the 09-26 codex job noted the same): every seat answer since 09-10 lives in the `note` prose, so `check_stage_panels.py`/section G cannot see any stage on this row.  Whether that is by design for research-only rows I did not establish; it is why item 2 could go unrecorded without a gate noticing.

Evidence basis: this seat alone, on the brief as posed; no other 7n seat answer was available to me.  Provisional on that basis per the carrier's provisional-adjudication rule, and every fact above that a gate or seat can re-run is named with its path.

#  ------------------------------------------------------------ vmedefaultkill (#568)
#  dev_vme.c's default: ended the emulator (exit(1), announced only by debug() -- silent while
#  free-running) on any VME2 offset the switch did not name.  Now: the 26 header-named registers
#  the model does not implement DECLINE (reads served the preloaded word, writes dropped, one
#  latched fatal() per register); every other unmatched offset returns 0, and memory_rw raises the
#  m88k data-access exception.  The rung-3 witness (regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/
#  witness_20261020/, 7 of 7 guest sites rc 1 on 5dbc000e) is NOT run here; this is its detector.
#  Needs only the binary: mvme88k/mvme187 constructs from the PROM, like the mk48/pcc2 blocks above.
VMEDLOG=$LOGDIR/vme_default.log
python3 -u vme_default_probe.py "$PMAX" gate > "$VMEDLOG" 2>&1 || true
if ! grep -q "VME_DEFAULT_PROBE_DONE" "$VMEDLOG"; then
    check "vme default probe completed" "no" "yes"
else
    check "vme default probe completed" "yes" "yes"
    check "vme default controls" "$(grep -c '^VME_DEFAULT_CONTROL=OK$' "$VMEDLOG")" "1"
    for v in "neg apparatus" "session" "live" "vbr reset" "t1cmp rt" "cased census" "free ctl" "free quiet" "free neg ctl" "tctl rt" "vbr rt" "t1count inc" "cased wr" "p2 rd census" "p2 wr census" "p2 ld widths" "p2 st widths" "p2 ann rd" "p2 ann rd 2" "p2 ann wr" "p2 ann wr 2" "p2 nw rd" "p2 nw wr" "p1 fault lo" "p1 fault hi" "p1 fault mid" "p1 fault wr" "p1 p2 edge" "p3 interim" "p2 int byte" "p2 int half" "p1 census" "p1 widths" "unasm"; do
        check "vme default row: $v" "$(count "$VMEDLOG" "^$v  .*ok$")" "1"
    done
    check "vme default rows emitted" "$(( $(count "$VMEDLOG" " ok$") + $(count "$VMEDLOG" " FAIL$") ))" "34"
    check "vme default rows green"   "$(count "$VMEDLOG" " ok$")"   "34"
    check "vme default rows red"     "$(count "$VMEDLOG" " FAIL$")" "0"
fi


#  ------------------------------------------------------------ hpcmipsctor (#444) + #536
#  Three of eight advertised hpcmips subtypes SIGABRTed during construction (#444).  Its rung-2
#  detector (~8 s, no rig image) shipped EXEMPT for want of a construction gate; #536 wires it here.
#  It ends by printing HPCMIPS_CTOR_RESULT=<ok>/<rows> and then its verdict, HPCMIPS_CTOR_PASS / _FAIL; the
#  RESULT line is the completion sentinel, so a run killed before its tally reds the first check.
#  C2 gets its unrelated-abort control from a LIVE, UNRELATED defect: mvme1600's construction aborts in
#  bus_pci_add() ("pci_data == NULL!"), and the row checks only the abort CLASS.  A round that fixes that
#  machine turns C2 red and owes its re-derivation -- the probe's own row F3 covers the classifier with a
#  synthetic abort.  Filed as `hpcmipsc2`.
HPCLOG=$LOGDIR/hpcmips_ctor.log
python3 -u hpcmips_ctor_probe.py --binary "$PMAX" > "$HPCLOG" 2>&1 || true
if ! grep -q "^HPCMIPS_CTOR_RESULT=" "$HPCLOG"; then
    check "hpcmips ctor probe completed" "no" "yes"
else
    check "hpcmips ctor probe completed" "yes" "yes"
    for v in P1 P2 P3 F1 F2 F3 F4 F5 E1 A1 A2 A3 A4 A5 B1 C1 C2 C3 C4 C5 S0 S1 S4 S5 S2 S3 R1 I1; do
        check "hpcmips ctor row: $v" "$(grep -c "^  \[ok\] $v " "$HPCLOG")" "1"
    done
    check "hpcmips ctor rows green" "$(grep -c '^  \[ok\] ' "$HPCLOG")"   "28"
    check "hpcmips ctor rows red"   "$(grep -c '^  \[FAIL\] ' "$HPCLOG")" "0"
    check "hpcmips ctor verdict"    "$(grep -c '^HPCMIPS_CTOR_PASS$' "$HPCLOG")" "1"
fi


PITLOG=$LOGDIR/pit8253_latch.log
python3 pit8253_latch_probe.py "$PMAX" "$STUB" > "$PITLOG" 2>&1 || true

#  A PRESERVED LOG IS VERIFIED BY ITS COMPLETION SENTINEL, NOT BY ITS EXISTENCE.  The probe
#  gained PIT8253_LATCH_DONE specifically so this row could be written correctly the first
#  time -- before it, the last line was a bare "PASS", a word that occurs in dozens of other
#  contexts and cannot distinguish a COMPLETED run from a TRUNCATED one.
if ! grep -q "PIT8253_LATCH_DONE" "$PITLOG"; then
    note "pit8253 probe did not reach its completion sentinel; last lines follow"
    tail -5 "$PITLOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
    check "pit8253 probe completed" "no" "yes"
else
    grep -E "^  (ok|FAIL) " "$PITLOG" | sed 's/^/       /'

    #  THE THREE CONTROLS PLUS THE MMU-ON NEGATIVE CONTROL, each pinned by name.  A count of
    #  green rows would let a control go red while the total stayed acceptable.
    for c in "C1 liveness" "C2 MMU state" "A1 device signature" "T  MMU-ON control"; do
        check "pit8253 control: $c" \
              "$(grep -c "^  ok   $c" "$PITLOG")" "1"
    done


